Young kids can't always be able to understand what they've seen, and it can be left rolling into your mind and anxious," she says.
However, if a child has come across television news images of refugees or heard about it from elsewhere, it's worth bringing it up.
It's a good idea to have a conversation about your child's premise and listen carefully to what he or she's asking.
A child's fears can be surprising.
They don't necessarily be the ones a parent thinks he's afraid.
You shouldn't intimidate a child for nothing, even if you're distressed and frightening yourself.
A smaller child is closely monitoring the behavior of their parents.
If the parents stay calm during the conversation, the child feels safe.
Older children are likely to come across a debate about refugees at school, with mates or online.
It's a good idea for your parents to bring things up with them if you're thinking about it.
The fact is that not all people in the world have things to do well.
Parents, together with their children, can think about how to deal with this in our family.
After all, do we find the world a safe or unsustainable place?
Older parents may also have different educational aspects - some may want to protect their children from anxiety where possible.
On the other hand: If the family also talks about difficult issues, the child gets a model of how to deal with the difficulties that come to life.
This could be of benefit to him in the future.
