4 killed by police shooting rubber bullets against protesters in Nepal
Four people, including a four-year-old boy, were killed in southern Nepal after police shot rubber bullets into a mocquary protesting against the country's new constitutional proposition, an official said on Wednesday.
Violence occurred Tuesday night in the Rupandeh region about 175 mile west of Katmandus, the capital.
Bishnu Prasad Dhakal, the area's top official, said more than 1,000 demonstrators had gathered outside the police station, and some of them threw rocks and fire bombs.
Dhakal said police were using tear gas and then firing in the air before resorting to rubber bullets.
Thirty police officers and five civilians were injured, he said.
It wasn't immediately clear why rubber bullets known to have been killed but traditionally used as a less dangerous option appeared to have resulted in so many deaths.
The clash was the latest in week-to-week violent demonstrations across the southern plains in Nepal where members of an Ethnic Group named Madhesi say new provincial provincial provinces established under the proposed new constitution are undermining their political empowerment.
