Calculate your own BMI here: 1.usa.gov/1D0ZqDv.
For this new study, researchers at Min-Sheng General Hospital in Taiwan used data collected since 2007 to compare two types of bariatric surgery, gastric by-rescue graft graft surgery, gastric bypass, and gastric reduction, with medical treatments for type 2 diabetes in people who were mildly obedient.
The study showed an average BMI for people underwent surgery fell from 31 to 24.5 by the end of the fifth year.
At the same time, BMI remained more or less the same for people treated by means other than surgery.
Thirty-six per cent of people who cut diabetes completely and 28% partially recovered.
